# The naive Dockerfile. Read it, then write a better one.
#
# This file is deliberately the version you are being asked to improve on. It
# builds, it runs, and it fails the acceptance criterion, which is the point:
# an image that works is not the same as an image you would deploy. Your own
# Dockerfile goes in this folder as `Dockerfile` and is assessed. This one is
# not read by Docker unless you name it.
#
# The build context is this folder, because everything the image needs is in
# it. The domain package is source in this project rather than an artefact
# somebody has to install first, so one Maven project builds the whole service:
#
#   docker build -f sprint-06-trade-api/Dockerfile.example \
#     -t trade-api:naive sprint-06-trade-api
#
# WHAT IS WRONG WITH THIS FILE
#
# 1. One stage. The image that runs the service is the image that built it, so
#    it ships Maven, a full JDK, the entire dependency cache and your source
#    code. That is several hundred megabytes of things the service never uses,
#    and every tool in an image is a tool available to whoever gets into the
#    container. A compiler in production is a compiler in production.
#
# 2. It runs as root. A process that never needs to write to the image should
#    not be able to.
#
# 3. `COPY . .` before the build means any change to any file in this folder
#    invalidates the layer holding the resolved dependencies, so every rebuild
#    re-downloads the world. It also copies anything git-ignored that happens
#    to be sitting there into the image.
#
# 4. The jar name is written out by hand, so a version bump in pom.xml produces
#    an image that builds and then fails to start.
#
# 5. Nothing tells Docker whether the service is healthy, so `docker compose ps`
#    reports a container that is running even when the application inside it
#    died on startup.

FROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-21

WORKDIR /build

COPY . .

RUN mvn -B -DskipTests package

EXPOSE 8080

CMD ["java", "-jar", "/build/target/trade-api-1.0.0.jar"]
